    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Advertising</span> is one of the most visible activities of commerce. Every day, businesses spend huge sums of money trying to inform and persuade the public about their goods and services. This topic introduces what advertising means, why businesses invest in it, and the different types of advertising used in commerce.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Advertising</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Advertising</span> is a paid, non-personal form of communication used by an identified sponsor (a business or organization) to inform, persuade, or remind the public about a product, service, or idea, usually through the mass media. It is one of the major <span class="learn-keyword">aids to trade</span> because it helps to bridge the gap between producers and consumers.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Importance of Advertising</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>To the Producer/Seller</th><th>To the Consumer</th><th>To the Economy</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Creates awareness of new products</td><td>Provides information for wise buying decisions</td><td>Creates employment (advertising agencies, media houses)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Increases sales and turnover</td><td>Educates on product uses and benefits</td><td>Encourages competition and quality improvement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Builds brand image and loyalty</td><td>Saves time in searching for products</td><td>Supports the mass media through advertising revenue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Helps fight competition</td><td>Introduces new products and alternatives</td><td>Stimulates mass production and lowers unit cost</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Types of Advertising</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Informative Advertising</td><td>Introduces a new product; gives facts about features/uses</td><td>Announcing the launch of a new phone model</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Persuasive Advertising</td><td>Convinces consumers to choose one brand over competitors</td><td>"Our detergent washes whiter than any other"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Competitive Advertising</td><td>Directly compares a product with rival brands to win customers</td><td>Two soft drink companies advertising against each other</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reminder Advertising</td><td>Keeps an already popular product in the consumer\'s mind</td><td>Repeated adverts for a well-known soap brand</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Features of a Good Advertisement</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A good advertisement should be truthful, clear, attractive, and able to catch attention quickly. It should state the benefits of the product, be appropriate for its target audience, and comply with advertising laws and ethics in Nigeria (regulated by bodies such as ARCON — the Advertising Regulatory Council of Nigeria).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse the three main types. Informative = "here is a new product"; Persuasive = "choose us over them"; Competitive = a direct, named comparison with a rival product.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Advertising is a paid, non-personal communication used to inform, persuade, or remind consumers about goods and services. It benefits producers, consumers, and the wider economy, and comes mainly in informative, persuasive, competitive, and reminder forms.\n</div>\n  ',
