    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Having learned what advertising is and its types, we now look at <span class="learn-keyword">media of advertising</span> — the channels businesses use to deliver their messages to the public — and weigh the strengths and weaknesses of each.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Advertising Media</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The <span class="learn-keyword">media of advertising</span> refers to the various channels or vehicles through which an advertisement is carried from the advertiser to the target audience. Media are broadly grouped into print, electronic, and outdoor media.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Advertising Media</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Print media</td><td>Newspapers, magazines, handbills, posters, brochures</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Electronic media</td><td>Television, radio, the internet/social media</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Outdoor (out-of-home) media</td><td>Billboards, banners, signposts, transit/vehicle adverts</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Advantages and Disadvantages of Each Medium</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Medium</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Newspaper/Magazine (Print)</td><td>Can be kept for reference; detailed information; targets specific readership</td><td>Requires literacy; limited to readers; can go stale quickly (newspapers)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Television</td><td>Combines sound, sight, and motion; wide reach; persuasive</td><td>Expensive; message is fleeting unless repeated; needs electricity/TV set</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Radio</td><td>Reaches illiterate audiences; relatively cheap; wide local reach</td><td>No visual element; message can be missed or forgotten quickly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Internet/Social Media</td><td>Wide and fast reach; interactive; can target specific audiences; relatively low cost</td><td>Requires internet access/data; risk of ad-blindness or being ignored; digital divide excludes some people</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Outdoor (billboards, posters)</td><td>Seen repeatedly by passers-by; long-lasting exposure in one location</td><td>Limited information can be shown; affected by weather; fixed location only</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Choosing the Right Medium</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The choice of advertising medium depends on the target audience, cost, nature of the product, and coverage needed. For example, a product targeted at rural, largely non-literate consumers may be better advertised on radio, while a technical product aimed at professionals may suit print or the internet.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners like to test which medium suits which audience. Remember: radio reaches non-literate audiences cheaply; television combines sound and pictures but costs more; the internet is interactive but excludes those without access.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Advertising media are grouped into print, electronic, and outdoor categories, each with distinct advantages and limitations. The right choice of medium depends on the target audience, cost, and the message to be communicated.\n</div>\n  ',
