    topic: 'Insurance',
    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-04',
    module: 'Risk Management in Commerce',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Business and everyday life involve risks — fire outbreaks, accidents, theft, illness, and death. <span class="learn-keyword">Insurance</span> is one of the most important aids to trade because it helps individuals and businesses manage and reduce the financial impact of such risks.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Insurance</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Insurance</span> is a contract in which a person or business (the <strong>insured</strong>) pays a regular sum of money (called a <strong>premium</strong>) to an insurance company (the <strong>insurer</strong>), which promises to compensate the insured for a specified loss, damage, illness, or death, should it occur.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Basic Principles of Insurance</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Utmost Good Faith</td><td>Both parties must disclose all relevant facts honestly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Insurable Interest</td><td>The insured must stand to suffer a genuine financial loss if the event occurs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Indemnity</td><td>Compensation should restore the insured to their original financial position, not more</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Contribution</td><td>Where a risk is insured with more than one insurer, each pays a proportionate share of the loss</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Subrogation</td><td>After compensating the insured, the insurer takes over the insured\'s legal rights to recover losses from a third party</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Proximate Cause</td><td>The insurer only pays for a loss whose immediate/direct cause is covered by the policy</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Types of Insurance</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What It Covers</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Life Insurance</td><td>Pays a sum on the death of the insured, or after a set period, to beneficiaries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Marine Insurance</td><td>Covers loss or damage to ships and cargo during sea transport</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fire Insurance</td><td>Covers loss or damage to property caused by fire</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Motor/Accident Insurance</td><td>Covers loss, damage, or injury arising from motor vehicle accidents</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of Insurance</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Insurance gives businesses and individuals financial security and peace of mind, encourages investment and risk-taking (since losses can be recovered), provides employment in the insurance industry, and mobilizes savings that can be invested in the economy.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse "indemnity" (restoring to original position) with "insurable interest" (having a genuine stake in what is being insured). These are commonly mixed up in exams.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Insurance is a contract where the insured pays a premium to the insurer for compensation against specified risks. It is guided by principles such as utmost good faith, insurable interest, and indemnity, and comes in types including life, marine, fire, and accident insurance.\n</div>\n  ',
