    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Banks sit at the heart of every modern economy, providing the financial services that make trade and commerce possible. This lesson introduces the meaning of a bank, the types of banks, and the roles commercial banks play in business transactions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of a Bank</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">bank</span> is a financial institution licensed to receive deposits from the public, provide loans, and offer other financial services such as payments and currency exchange.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Banks</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Main Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Central Bank</td><td>Regulates and supervises all other banks; issues currency; controls monetary policy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Commercial Banks</td><td>Accept deposits, give loans, and provide day-to-day banking services to individuals and businesses</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Development Banks</td><td>Provide long-term finance for specific sectors like agriculture, industry, or housing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Merchant Banks</td><td>Provide services mainly to large businesses — corporate finance, underwriting, advisory</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Microfinance Banks</td><td>Provide small-scale loans and financial services to low-income individuals and small businesses</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Roles of Commercial Banks in Business Transactions</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Accepting deposits</td><td>Provides a safe place for individuals and businesses to keep money</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Granting loans and overdrafts</td><td>Provides finance for business expansion and working capital</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Facilitating payments</td><td>Allows payments via cheques, transfers, ATMs, and POS terminals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Foreign exchange services</td><td>Enables businesses to conduct international trade transactions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Safekeeping of valuables</td><td>Offers safe deposit boxes for important documents and valuables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Financial advice</td><td>Advises businesses on investment and financial planning</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Types of Bank Accounts</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Commercial banks offer different types of accounts including <strong>current accounts</strong> (for frequent transactions, often with cheque facilities), <strong>savings accounts</strong> (for accumulating savings, usually earning interest), and <strong>fixed deposit accounts</strong> (money kept for a fixed period at a higher interest rate).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse commercial banks (everyday banking for the public) with development banks (long-term finance for specific sectors) or merchant banks (corporate/large-business services).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> A bank is a financial institution that accepts deposits and provides loans and other services. Nigeria has several types of banks including central, commercial, development, merchant, and microfinance banks, with commercial banks playing a central role in facilitating everyday business transactions.\n</div>\n  ',
