    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Having learned about commercial banks, we now turn to the <span class="learn-keyword">Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)</span> — the apex financial institution that regulates and controls the entire banking system.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of the Central Bank of Nigeria</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The <span class="learn-keyword">Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)</span> is Nigeria\'s apex monetary authority, established to regulate the banking industry, manage the currency, and implement monetary policy on behalf of the government.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sole issuer of currency</td><td>Only the CBN has the legal right to print and issue the Naira</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Banker to the government</td><td>Manages government accounts and provides banking services to the federal government</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Banker\'s bank</td><td>Holds reserves for and lends to commercial banks; acts as lender of last resort</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Regulator/supervisor of banks</td><td>Licenses, monitors, and enforces rules on commercial and other banks</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Controller of monetary policy</td><td>Manages interest rates, money supply, and inflation to stabilize the economy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Custodian of external reserves</td><td>Manages the country\'s foreign exchange reserves</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Advisor to government</td><td>Advises government on economic and financial matters</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. How the CBN Controls Commercial Banks</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>How It Works</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Monetary Policy Rate (MPR)</td><td>Sets the benchmark interest rate that influences borrowing costs across the economy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)</td><td>Requires banks to keep a percentage of deposits with the CBN, limiting how much they can lend</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Open Market Operations</td><td>Buying/selling government securities to control the amount of money in circulation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Licensing and supervision</td><td>Grants and can revoke banking licenses; conducts regular examinations of banks</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moral suasion</td><td>Persuades banks to align with government economic policy through guidance rather than force</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Why the Central Bank Is Important</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The CBN maintains the stability and soundness of the financial system, controls inflation, protects depositors by supervising banks, and ensures the Naira retains value both locally and internationally.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the phrase "lender of last resort" — this means the CBN lends to commercial banks when no one else will, to prevent a bank from collapsing and destabilizing the financial system.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Central Bank of Nigeria is the apex regulatory institution responsible for issuing currency, controlling monetary policy, supervising commercial banks, and maintaining overall financial stability in the country.\n</div>\n  ',
