    topic: 'Banking III',
    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-07',
    module: 'Banking and Finance',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Beyond commercial and central banks, Nigeria\'s financial system includes <span class="learn-keyword">development banks</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">merchant banks</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">microfinance banks</span> — each serving a distinct segment of the economy and contributing uniquely to economic growth.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Development Banks</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Development banks</span> provide long-term finance to specific sectors of the economy that may be underserved by commercial banks, such as agriculture, industry, and housing. Examples in Nigeria include the Bank of Industry (BOI), Bank of Agriculture (BOA), and the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN).</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Bank</th><th>Sector Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Bank of Industry (BOI)</td><td>Industrial and manufacturing projects</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bank of Agriculture (BOA)</td><td>Agricultural development and farming projects</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)</td><td>Housing and mortgage finance</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Merchant Banks</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Merchant banks</span> mainly serve corporate clients (large businesses and government), offering services such as corporate finance, underwriting of shares and bonds, mergers and acquisitions advisory, and investment management. They generally do not deal with everyday retail customers.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Microfinance Banks</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Microfinance banks</span> provide financial services — small loans, savings, and micro-insurance — to low-income individuals, market women, artisans, and small business owners who typically lack access to traditional commercial banking due to insufficient collateral or documentation.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Contributions to Economic Growth</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Bank Type</th><th>Contribution to Growth</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Development banks</td><td>Fund large, long-term projects that boost industrialization, agriculture, and infrastructure</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Merchant banks</td><td>Help large businesses raise capital, enabling expansion and job creation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Microfinance banks</td><td>Empower small businesses and reduce poverty by improving financial inclusion at the grassroots level</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember specific Nigerian examples: BOI (industry), BOA (agriculture), FMBN (housing/mortgage) — these are commonly tested development banks.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Development banks provide long-term sector-specific finance, merchant banks serve large corporate clients, and microfinance banks serve low-income individuals and small businesses. Together, they complement commercial banks and contribute significantly to Nigeria\'s economic growth and financial inclusion.\n</div>\n  ',
