    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-09',
    module: 'Business Finance',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Every business needs money to start, run, and grow. This money and the resources it buys are collectively known as <span class="learn-keyword">capital</span>. Understanding the types and importance of capital is essential to understanding how businesses are financed.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Capital</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Capital</span> refers to the money, assets, or resources used by a business to start, operate, and expand its activities. In commerce, capital is the wealth invested in a business to generate more wealth (profit).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Capital</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fixed capital</td><td>Money invested in long-term assets used repeatedly in the business (e.g., land, buildings, machinery)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Working capital</td><td>Money available for the day-to-day running of the business (e.g., paying for stock, wages, utility bills)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Liquid capital</td><td>Capital held in cash or assets that can be quickly converted to cash</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Loan capital</td><td>Money borrowed from banks or other lenders to finance the business, repayable with interest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Owner\'s/Equity capital</td><td>Money contributed by the owner(s) or shareholders of the business</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Sources of Business Capital</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Capital can be sourced from personal savings, family and friends, bank loans, sale of shares (for companies), retained profits, cooperative societies, and government grants or loan schemes.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of Capital to Business</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Importance</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Starting the business</td><td>Capital is required to set up premises, purchase equipment, and buy initial stock</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Running operations</td><td>Working capital keeps day-to-day activities going (paying staff, buying supplies)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Expansion</td><td>Additional capital allows a business to grow, open new branches, or diversify</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Meeting emergencies</td><td>Liquid capital cushions the business against unexpected expenses</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Competitive advantage</td><td>Well-capitalized businesses can invest in better technology and marketing</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse fixed capital (long-term assets like machinery) with working capital (money for daily operations like buying stock or paying wages) — this distinction is frequently tested.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Capital is the money or resources used to start, run, and grow a business. It comes in types such as fixed, working, liquid, loan, and owner\'s capital, and is essential for starting operations, day-to-day running, expansion, and building competitive advantage.\n</div>\n  ',
