    topic: 'Credit',
    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-10',
    module: 'Business Finance',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Not every business transaction is settled with immediate cash payment. <span class="learn-keyword">Credit</span> allows goods, services, or money to be exchanged now with payment deferred to a later date — a practice that oils the wheels of modern commerce.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Credit</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Credit</span> refers to an arrangement in which a buyer receives goods, services, or money now, with an agreement to pay for them at a later date. The person or business granting the credit is the <strong>creditor</strong>, while the one receiving it is the <strong>debtor</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Credit</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Trade credit</td><td>Goods supplied by one business to another with payment deferred to an agreed date</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bank credit</td><td>Loans, overdrafts, or other credit facilities extended by a bank to a customer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hire purchase</td><td>A buyer takes possession of goods immediately and pays for them in installments, with ownership transferring only after full payment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consumer credit</td><td>Credit extended to individual consumers to purchase goods or services for personal use</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Advantages of Credit</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Advantage</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Boosts sales</td><td>Encourages customers to buy more than they could afford with immediate cash</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Helps cash flow</td><td>Allows businesses to acquire goods or equipment without paying the full amount upfront</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Builds business relationships</td><td>Strengthens trust and long-term relationships between trading partners</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enables large purchases</td><td>Allows consumers to acquire expensive items (like vehicles) they could not pay for outright</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Disadvantages of Credit</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Credit can lead to bad debts if debtors fail to pay, ties up a creditor\'s capital until payment is received, may encourage overspending or reckless buying, and often involves added costs (interest charges) for the debtor.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: the creditor gives credit (is owed money); the debtor receives credit (owes money). Students commonly mix these two up in exams.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Credit is an arrangement allowing goods, services, or money to be received now with payment deferred to later. It comes in types such as trade credit, bank credit, hire purchase, and consumer credit, and offers advantages like boosting sales but also carries risks such as bad debts.\n</div>\n  ',
