    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Why do people go into business? For most, the answer is <span class="learn-keyword">profit</span>. This lesson examines what profit means, its types, and the factors that determine how much profit a business makes.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Profit</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Profit</span> is the financial gain a business makes when its total revenue (sales income) exceeds its total costs (expenses) over a given period. It is the reward for taking on the risk of running a business.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Profit</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Gross profit</td><td>Sales revenue minus the cost of goods sold, before deducting other business expenses</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Net profit</td><td>Gross profit minus all other operating expenses (rent, salaries, utilities, etc.)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Normal profit</td><td>The minimum profit needed to keep a business owner motivated to continue operating in that line of business</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Abnormal/Super-normal profit</td><td>Profit earned above the normal profit level, often due to favorable market conditions</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Profit to Business</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Importance</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reward for risk-taking</td><td>Compensates the entrepreneur for the risk and effort of running the business</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Source of funds for expansion</td><td>Profit can be reinvested (retained profit) to grow the business</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Measure of performance</td><td>Indicates how well a business is being managed and how efficiently it operates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Attracts investment</td><td>Profitable businesses attract investors and are more easily able to borrow</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enables tax and dividend payments</td><td>Profits allow a business to pay taxes to government and dividends to shareholders</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Factors Affecting Profit</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Profit is affected by factors such as the level of sales/turnover, cost of production, competition in the market, pricing strategy, efficiency of management, and general economic conditions (e.g., inflation, exchange rates).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse gross profit (before other expenses) with net profit (after all expenses are deducted). Net profit is always lower than gross profit unless there are no other expenses at all.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Profit is the surplus of revenue over cost, and is the main motivation for engaging in business. It comes in types including gross, net, normal, and abnormal profit, and is affected by factors like sales levels, costs, competition, and management efficiency.\n</div>\n  ',
