    topic: 'Turnover',
    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-12',
    module: 'Business Finance',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Turnover</span> is one of the most commonly used measures of business activity. This lesson explains what turnover means, how it is calculated, and why it matters to businesses of all sizes.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Turnover</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Turnover</span> refers to the total value of sales made by a business over a specific period, usually a month or a year. It is also called <strong>sales revenue</strong> or <strong>gross revenue</strong>, and it measures the scale of a business\'s trading activity before any costs are deducted.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Calculating Turnover</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Turnover is calculated by multiplying the quantity of goods sold by the selling price per unit:</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Formula</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Turnover = Quantity Sold × Selling Price per Unit</td><td>500 units × ₦2,000 = ₦1,000,000 turnover</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p">Note that turnover is different from profit — turnover is the total income from sales, while profit is what remains after all costs have been deducted from turnover.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Turnover in Business Operations</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Importance</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Measures business size/scale</td><td>A high turnover often indicates a larger or more active business</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Basis for tax assessment</td><td>Government tax authorities may use turnover to assess certain business taxes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Attracts investors and lenders</td><td>A strong, growing turnover can make a business more attractive for investment or loans</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Indicates market demand</td><td>Rising turnover suggests increasing demand for the business\'s goods or services</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Used in performance comparison</td><td>Businesses compare turnover year-on-year, or against competitors, to assess growth</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Turnover vs Profit</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A business can have a high turnover but low or no profit if its costs are also high. For example, a supermarket may have a very high turnover due to large sales volumes but a relatively low profit margin per item sold, while a small jewelry shop might have low turnover but a high profit margin.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Never confuse turnover with profit. Turnover = total sales revenue; Profit = what is left after costs are deducted from turnover. A business can have high turnover and low profit, or vice versa.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Turnover is the total value of a business\'s sales over a given period, calculated as quantity sold multiplied by selling price. It measures business scale and activity, is important for tax and investment purposes, but is distinct from profit.\n</div>\n  ',
