    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Every business transaction is governed by rules. <span class="learn-keyword">Business law</span> provides the legal framework within which trade and commerce operate, particularly through the law of contract, which governs agreements between parties.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Importance of Business Law</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Business law</span> refers to the body of laws that govern business transactions, protecting the rights of parties involved and ensuring fairness and order in commercial dealings. It is important because it provides legal certainty, protects both buyers and sellers, and provides a means of resolving disputes.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Meaning of a Contract</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">contract</span> is a legally binding agreement between two or more parties that creates rights and obligations which the law will enforce.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Essential Elements of a Valid Contract</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Offer</td><td>A clear proposal by one party to enter into an agreement on specific terms</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>The other party\'s unqualified agreement to the exact terms of the offer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consideration</td><td>Something of value (money, goods, service) exchanged by each party</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Capacity</td><td>Parties must be legally competent (of sound mind, of age, not otherwise restricted)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Intention to create legal relations</td><td>Both parties must intend the agreement to be legally binding</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Legality of purpose</td><td>The subject matter of the contract must not be illegal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Free consent</td><td>Agreement must be given freely, without fraud, duress, or undue influence</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Rights and Obligations of Parties to a Contract</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Once a valid contract is formed, each party has a <strong>right</strong> to expect the other to perform their part of the agreement, and a corresponding <strong>obligation</strong> to fulfill their own promises. If a party fails to perform their obligations without lawful excuse, this is called a <strong>breach of contract</strong>, and the injured party may seek legal remedies such as damages (compensation) or specific performance.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn the essential elements of a valid contract by heart — offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, intention to create legal relations, legality, and free consent. Missing any one of these can make a contract void or voidable.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Business law governs commercial transactions, with the law of contract at its core. A valid contract requires offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, intention to create legal relations, legality, and free consent — and creates enforceable rights and obligations for both parties.\n</div>\n  ',
