    topic: 'Structure of Business',
    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-14',
    module: 'Forms of Business Organization',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Businesses in commerce can be organized in different legal and structural forms, each with its own advantages and disadvantages. This topic examines the major types of business organizations found in Nigeria.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Sole Proprietorship</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">sole proprietorship</span> is a business owned and controlled by one person, who provides the capital, makes all decisions, and takes all the profit — but also bears all the risk, including <strong>unlimited liability</strong> for the business\'s debts.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Partnership</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">partnership</span> is a business owned by two or more people (usually 2–20) who agree to share capital, management, profits, and losses, typically guided by a partnership agreement (deed).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Companies</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Private limited company</td><td>Owned by shareholders (2–50); shares cannot be sold to the general public; limited liability</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Public limited company</td><td>Can sell shares to the general public through the stock exchange; limited liability; often has many shareholders</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p">A company is a separate legal entity from its owners, meaning it can own property, sue, and be sued in its own name, and its shareholders enjoy <strong>limited liability</strong> — they can only lose what they have invested.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Cooperatives</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">cooperative society</span> is a voluntary association of individuals who come together to pool resources, save, and provide services (such as loans) to members for their mutual benefit, operating on democratic principles (one member, one vote).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Comparing the Forms of Business</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Ownership</th><th>Liability</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sole proprietorship</td><td>One person</td><td>Unlimited</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partnership</td><td>2–20 people (general)</td><td>Usually unlimited (unless limited partnership)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Private limited company</td><td>2–50 shareholders</td><td>Limited</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Public limited company</td><td>Unlimited shareholders (public)</td><td>Limited</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cooperative society</td><td>Voluntary members</td><td>Usually limited to member contributions</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> "Unlimited liability" means personal assets can be seized to pay business debts (sole proprietorship, ordinary partnership). "Limited liability" means owners can only lose what they invested (companies).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Businesses can be structured as sole proprietorships, partnerships, private or public limited companies, or cooperative societies, each differing in ownership, liability, and legal status.\n</div>\n  ',
