    module: 'Business Management',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Having covered planning and organizing, we now examine the remaining core functions of management: <span class="learn-keyword">directing</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">coordinating</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">controlling</span> — the functions that bring plans to life and keep the business on track.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Directing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Directing</span> is the management function that involves leading, guiding, motivating, and supervising employees to carry out their assigned tasks in order to achieve business objectives. It includes giving instructions, communicating effectively, and inspiring staff to perform well.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element of Directing</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Leadership</td><td>Guiding and influencing employees toward achieving goals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Motivation</td><td>Encouraging employees to perform at their best (e.g., through rewards, recognition)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Communication</td><td>Clearly conveying instructions, expectations, and feedback</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Supervision</td><td>Overseeing employees\' work to ensure tasks are done correctly</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Coordinating</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Coordinating</span> ensures that the activities of different individuals and departments are harmonized and work together smoothly toward the overall goals of the business, avoiding duplication, conflict, or wasted effort.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Controlling</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Controlling</span> is the process of monitoring business performance, comparing actual results against set objectives or standards, and taking corrective action where necessary.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Step in Controlling</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Setting standards</td><td>Establishing benchmarks against which performance will be measured</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Measuring performance</td><td>Tracking actual results achieved</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Comparing results to standards</td><td>Identifying any gap between actual and expected performance</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Taking corrective action</td><td>Making changes to address any shortfalls identified</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Why These Functions Matter Together</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Directing ensures employees are motivated and know what to do; coordinating ensures everyone\'s efforts fit together; and controlling ensures the business stays on track toward its goals, making corrections when things go off course. Together with planning and organizing, these five functions (often summarized as POCCC) make up the complete management process.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Controlling is not about punishing staff — it\'s about comparing actual results to set standards and correcting deviations. Directing is about leading and motivating people, not just giving orders.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Directing involves leading, motivating, and supervising employees. Coordinating ensures departments and individuals work together harmoniously. Controlling involves monitoring performance against standards and taking corrective action. Together with planning and organizing, these form the complete management process.\n</div>\n  ',
