    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Many people confuse "marketing" with "selling," but marketing is a much broader concept. This lesson introduces the meaning and importance of marketing, and clarifies how it differs from selling.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Marketing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Marketing</span> is the process of identifying, anticipating, and satisfying customer needs and wants profitably. It covers everything from researching what customers want, developing the right product, pricing it appropriately, promoting it, and delivering it to where customers can buy it.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Importance of Marketing</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Importance</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Identifies customer needs</td><td>Helps businesses understand what customers actually want before producing goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Guides product development</td><td>Ensures products are designed to meet real market demand</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Creates value and satisfaction</td><td>Ensures customers get products/services that genuinely meet their needs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Boosts sales and profitability</td><td>Effective marketing leads to increased sales and business growth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Builds brand loyalty</td><td>Good marketing fosters long-term relationships between businesses and customers</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Marketing Mix (The 4 Ps)</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Product</td><td>The goods or services offered to meet customer needs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Price</td><td>The amount customers must pay to acquire the product</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Place</td><td>The channels through which the product reaches the customer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Promotion</td><td>The methods used to inform and persuade customers about the product</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Difference Between Marketing and Selling</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Marketing</th><th>Selling</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Starts with identifying customer needs before production</td><td>Starts with an existing product that needs to be sold</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Broad process: research, product development, pricing, promotion, distribution</td><td>Narrow process: focused mainly on persuading customers to buy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Long-term focus on customer satisfaction and relationship</td><td>Short-term focus on completing the immediate transaction</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Views the customer as the starting point</td><td>Views the product as the starting point</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: "Selling" is just one activity WITHIN marketing (part of promotion). Marketing is the whole process — from understanding customer needs to delivering the finished product and beyond.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Marketing is the broad process of identifying and satisfying customer needs profitably, guided by the marketing mix (Product, Price, Place, Promotion). Unlike selling, which focuses narrowly on persuading customers to buy an existing product, marketing starts with understanding the customer.\n</div>\n  ',
