    module: 'Consumer Welfare',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Consumers can be exploited through overpricing, adulterated goods, false advertising, and unsafe products. <span class="learn-keyword">Consumer protection</span> exists to guard against these abuses and ensure fairness in the marketplace.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Consumer Protection</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Consumer protection</span> refers to the laws, regulations, and organizations put in place to safeguard the rights and interests of buyers of goods and services, and to ensure fair trade practices in the market.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Basic Rights of a Consumer</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Right</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Right to safety</td><td>Protection against products and services that are hazardous to health or life</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Right to information</td><td>Access to accurate information needed to make informed buying decisions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Right to choose</td><td>Access to a variety of goods and services at competitive prices</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Right to be heard</td><td>Ability to voice complaints and have consumer interests considered in policy-making</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Right to redress</td><td>Ability to seek fair compensation for misrepresentation, poor quality goods, or unsatisfactory services</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Right to consumer education</td><td>Access to knowledge that helps consumers make informed decisions and know their rights</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Ways Consumers Are Exploited</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Consumers can be exploited through overpricing, sale of adulterated or fake goods, false or misleading advertising, use of inaccurate weights and measures, hoarding to create artificial scarcity, and selling of substandard or expired products.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Why Consumer Protection Matters</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Consumer protection builds trust between businesses and buyers, ensures fair competition among producers, protects public health and safety, and helps maintain confidence in the marketplace, which ultimately benefits both consumers and honest businesses.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn all six basic consumer rights by name — safety, information, choice, to be heard, redress, and consumer education. Exams often ask you to identify or match these rights to scenarios.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Consumer protection safeguards buyers through rights such as safety, information, choice, being heard, redress, and education. It exists because consumers can be exploited through overpricing, fake goods, false advertising, and other unfair practices.\n</div>\n  ',
