    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Beyond knowing consumer rights, it is important to know which organizations and laws exist in Nigeria to enforce and protect those rights. This lesson examines the key consumer protection agencies and their functions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Government Agencies for Consumer Protection in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Agency</th><th>Main Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Federal Competition and Consumer Protection Commission (FCCPC)</td><td>Protects consumer rights, regulates fair trade practices, and investigates consumer complaints</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control (NAFDAC)</td><td>Regulates and controls the quality of food, drugs, cosmetics, and related products</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON)</td><td>Sets and enforces quality standards for products manufactured or imported into Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consumer Protection Council (predecessor to FCCPC)</td><td>Historically handled consumer complaints and protection before being succeeded by the FCCPC</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)</td><td>Protects consumers of financial/banking services through regulation</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. How These Agencies Protect Consumers</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">These agencies enforce quality and safety standards, investigate and penalize businesses that engage in fraudulent or exploitative practices, educate the public about their rights, recall unsafe or substandard products from the market, and provide channels through which consumers can lodge complaints.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Role of the Consumer Themselves</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Consumers also have a responsibility to protect themselves by reading product labels, checking expiry dates, verifying NAFDAC/SON registration numbers on products, comparing prices, and reporting exploitative practices to the appropriate authorities.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of These Protective Measures</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Strong consumer protection agencies help maintain public confidence in the marketplace, protect public health (especially through the regulation of food and drugs), promote fair competition, and hold businesses accountable for their products and practices.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the specific roles: NAFDAC = food/drugs/cosmetics; SON = general product quality standards; FCCPC = broader consumer rights and fair trade. These are frequently tested individually.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigeria has several agencies dedicated to protecting consumers, including the FCCPC, NAFDAC, and SON, each with specific roles in enforcing standards, regulating products, and handling consumer complaints. Consumers themselves also play a role in protecting their own interests.\n</div>\n  ',
