    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Every purchase requires a method of payment. As commerce has modernized, the ways businesses and consumers settle transactions have expanded far beyond physical cash. This lesson introduces the meaning of payment and traditional methods used in trade.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Payment</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Payment</span> refers to the transfer of money (or its equivalent) from a buyer to a seller in exchange for goods or services received, settling an obligation arising from a transaction.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Traditional/Cash-Based Methods of Payment</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cash payment</td><td>Direct exchange of physical currency (notes and coins) for goods or services</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cheque</td><td>A written, signed instruction to a bank to pay a specified sum from the drawer\'s account to a named person</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Money order/Postal order</td><td>A document purchased from a post office guaranteeing payment of a specific amount to a named recipient</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bank draft</td><td>A cheque guaranteed by a bank, drawn on the bank\'s own funds rather than the customer\'s account directly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trade credit</td><td>Goods received now with payment agreed to be made at a later date</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Advantages and Disadvantages of Cash Payment</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Immediate, universally accepted</td><td>Risk of theft or loss</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>No transaction fees or processing delays</td><td>Inconvenient and risky for large sums</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Simple and requires no special equipment</td><td>No formal record of the transaction unless a receipt is given</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Why Cheques Are Useful</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Cheques allow large sums to be transferred safely without carrying physical cash, provide a written record of payment, and can be post-dated for future payment. However, they require both parties to have bank accounts, and there can be delays before funds are actually credited (a process called "clearing").</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the difference between a cheque (drawn on the customer\'s own account) and a bank draft (guaranteed and drawn on the bank\'s own funds, making it more secure for the recipient).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Payment is the transfer of money in exchange for goods or services. Traditional methods of payment include cash, cheques, money/postal orders, bank drafts, and trade credit, each with distinct features, advantages, and disadvantages.\n</div>\n  ',
