    topic: 'Deregulation, Nationalization, Indigenisation I',
    topicCode: 'SS2-COM-22',
    module: 'Government and Business',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Governments intervene in business activity in various ways. Three important policy tools are <span class="learn-keyword">deregulation</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">nationalization</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">indigenisation</span> — each reflecting a different approach to how much control the state exercises over commerce and industry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Deregulation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Deregulation</span> refers to the reduction or removal of government rules, controls, and restrictions on business activities in a particular industry, allowing market forces to operate more freely.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Meaning of Nationalization</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Nationalization</span> is the process by which a government takes ownership and control of private businesses or industries, transferring them from private to public (state) ownership.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Meaning of Indigenisation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Indigenisation</span> is a policy requiring that ownership and control of businesses within a country be transferred, at least in part, from foreign investors to the citizens (indigenes) of that country. In Nigeria, this was notably pursued through the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decrees of 1972 and 1977.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Comparing the Three Policies</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Main Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Deregulation</td><td>Reducing government control/rules over an industry to increase market freedom</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nationalization</td><td>Transferring ownership from private hands to government (public) ownership</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Indigenisation</td><td>Transferring ownership from foreign investors to local/national citizens</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Why Governments Pursue These Policies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Deregulation is often pursued to boost competition and efficiency. Nationalization is often pursued to protect strategic industries, redistribute wealth, or correct perceived market failures. Indigenisation is often pursued to increase local ownership, control, and participation in the national economy, reducing dependence on foreign investors.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse nationalization (private → government) with indigenisation (foreign → local/national citizens). Both can happen at once, but they answer different questions: WHO owns it (government vs. private) and WHERE the owners are from (foreign vs. local).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Deregulation reduces government control over an industry, nationalization transfers private businesses to government ownership, and indigenisation transfers foreign-owned businesses to local citizens. Nigeria notably pursued indigenisation policies in the 1970s.\n</div>\n  ',
