    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Having defined deregulation, nationalization, and indigenisation, we now examine their real effects on the Nigerian economy, including specific historical examples and the advantages and disadvantages of each policy.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Effects of Deregulation on the Nigerian Economy</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Positive Effects</th><th>Negative Effects</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Increased competition and efficiency (e.g., telecoms sector after GSM deregulation)</td><td>Can lead to job losses in previously protected industries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>More investment and innovation from private players</td><td>May initially cause price volatility as markets adjust</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Improved quality and variety of goods/services for consumers</td><td>Weaker businesses may struggle or collapse under new competition</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Effects of Nationalization on the Nigerian Economy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nigeria nationalized aspects of its oil industry in the 1970s (e.g., government stakes in oil companies through the NNPC). This gave the government greater control over a strategically important resource and revenue, but state-run enterprises have also often faced criticism for inefficiency, corruption, and poor service delivery compared to well-run private firms.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Effects of Indigenisation on the Nigerian Economy</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Positive Effects</th><th>Negative Effects</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Increased Nigerian ownership and participation in the economy</td><td>Some foreign investors withdrew or reduced investment, fearing loss of control</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Created opportunities for Nigerian entrepreneurs and investors</td><td>Some indigenised businesses lacked the technical expertise previously provided by foreign owners, affecting performance</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Encouraged local capital market development (more Nigerians buying shares)</td><td>Could discourage future foreign direct investment if seen as overly restrictive</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Balancing These Policies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Modern economic policy in Nigeria and elsewhere often seeks a balance: deregulating certain sectors to boost efficiency and competition, while still protecting strategic national interests, and encouraging local participation without discouraging much-needed foreign investment and expertise.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A commonly cited Nigerian example of deregulation is the telecommunications/GSM sector; a commonly cited example of indigenisation is the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decrees of the 1970s. Learn both.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Deregulation, nationalization, and indigenisation have each had significant, mixed effects on the Nigerian economy — boosting competition and local participation in some cases, while sometimes causing inefficiency, job losses, or reduced foreign investment. Effective policy generally seeks a balance between these approaches.\n</div>\n  ',
